.TH "zelta\-policy" "8" "" "" "System Manager\(cqs Manual"
.SH NAME
\f[B]zelta policy\f[R] \- execute a list of \f[B]zelta backup\f[R]
operations from a policy configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]zelta policy\f[R] [\f[I]backup\-override\-options\f[R]]
[\f[I]site\f[R]|\f[I]host\f[R]|\f[I]dataset\f[R]] \f[I]\&...\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]zelta policy\f[R] reads from a simple YAML\-style configuration
file containing parameters for \f[B]zelta backup\f[R].
Running \f[B]zelta policy\f[R] without parameters will iterate through
the entire configuration file.
Override options for either \f[B]zelta policy\f[R] or \f[B]zelta
backup\f[R] can be provided on the command line, for example, adding
\f[CR]\-\-json\f[R] will cause that parameter to be passed to all
\f[B]zelta backup\f[R] calls.
Subsequent undashed parameters will be matched against a user\-defined
\f[I]site\f[R] name, \f[I]host\f[R] name, or \f[I]dataset\f[R], and the
backup processes will be limited to that group or item.
.SS Options
In the \f[B]zelta policy\f[R] configuration file, you may override
\f[B]zelta backup\f[R]\(cqs default parameters as defined in
\f[B]zelta\-backup(8)\f[R].
For the complete list of Zelta configuration options, see
\f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R].
The following parameters are specific \f[B]zelta policy\f[R]:
.TP
\f[B]\-\-retry\f[R]
Retry failed syncs the indicated number of times.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-jobs\f[R]
Run the indicated number of policy jobs concurrently, one for each Site
in the configuration.
.TP
\f[B]\-n\f[R], \f[B]\-\-dryrun\f[R]
Show a table of source and target endpoints that would be processed,
then exit.
No backup jobs are run.
See \f[B]Dry Run Output\f[R] below.
.TP
\f[B]\-H\f[R]
Scripting mode: suppress the \f[B]SOURCE\f[R]/\f[B]TARGET\f[R] header
and separate columns with a single space.
Intended for pipeline use.
Only meaningful with \f[B]\(endryrun\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-C\f[R], \f[B]\-\-config\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Read policy configuration from \f[I]FILE\f[R] instead of the default
location.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-backup\-root\f[R]
The relative target path for the target job.
For example `bkhost:tank/Backups' would place backups below that dataset
(if not overridden).
.TP
\f[B]\-\-archive\-root\f[R]
Default archive target root for policy jobs that use archive\-style
destinations.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-backup\-command\f[R] \f[I]COMMAND\f[R]
Command used by policy when executing backup jobs.
This is primarily useful for wrappers and testing.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-host\-prefix\f[R]
Include the source hostname as a parent of the synced target, for
example, `tank/Backups/source.host/backup\-dataset'.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-ds\-prefix\f[R]
Similar to `zfs recv \-d' and `\-e', include the indicated number of
parent labels for the target\(cqs backup name.
See \f[B]zelta\-options(7)\f[R] for more detail.
.SS Import Files
Policy files may use \f[CR]import:\f[R] to insert another local policy
fragment before parsing.
Import paths are resolved relative to the file that contains the
\f[CR]import:\f[R] line, which allows a policy directory to be moved as
a unit.
.IP
.EX
SITE0\f[B]:\f[R]
  host1.example\f[B]:\f[R]
    options\f[B]:\f[R]
      import\f[B]:\f[R] targets/vault1.yaml
      import\f[B]:\f[R] rules/hostbackup.yaml
    datasets\f[B]:\f[R]
      import\f[B]:\f[R] sources/host1.example.yaml
.EE
.PP
Imported files are textual fragments, not independent policy files.
The indentation of the \f[CR]import:\f[R] line is prepended to each
imported line, so fragments should usually contain only the lines needed
inside the current context.
For example, a dataset inventory fragment can contain only list items:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]\-\f[R] tank/vm/app1
\f[B]\-\f[R] tank/vm/app2
.EE
.PP
Imports are expanded recursively up to a fixed depth limit, and
recursive import loops are rejected.
Later options override earlier options, but unspecified options remain
in effect within the current policy context.
.SS Dry Run Output
\f[B]zelta policy \(endryrun\f[R] prints a column\-aligned table of
fully\-resolved source and target endpoints, one row per backup job,
then exits without running any jobs:
.IP
.EX
SOURCE                                     TARGET
compute1.nyc1:ssd09/jail/bellmgmt_bts      vault1.den0:data1/Backups/bellmgmt_bts
compute1.nyc1:ssd09/jail/bellmgmt_bts      vault1.nyc1:rust12/Backups/bellmgmt_bts
.EE
.PP
Column width is computed dynamically from the longest source endpoint.
When a single source fans out to multiple targets, each target appears
as a separate row.
.PP
Adding \f[B]\(enverbose\f[R] (\f[CR]\-v\f[R]) switches to raw \f[B]zelta
backup\f[R] command output suitable for manual execution or debugging.
Policy\-internal environment variables (\f[B]ZELTA_LOG_PREFIX\f[R],
\f[B]ZELTA_LOG_MODE\f[R], \f[B]ZELTA_LOG_LEVEL\f[R],
\f[B]ZELTA_LOG_COMMAND\f[R]) are stripped from each command since they
are only meaningful within the policy process.
.PP
Adding \f[B]\-H\f[R] suppresses the \f[B]SOURCE\f[R]/\f[B]TARGET\f[R]
header and separates columns with a single space, making the output
suitable for scripting:
.IP
.EX
compute1.nyc1:ssd09/jail/bellmgmt_bts vault1.den0:data1/Backups/bellmgmt_bts
compute1.nyc1:ssd09/jail/bellmgmt_bts vault1.nyc1:rust12/Backups/bellmgmt_bts
.EE
.PP
All three modes compose with operand filtering.
For example, \f[CR]zelta policy \-n bellmgmt_bts\f[R] shows only the
rows matching that dataset.
.SS Backup Job Parameters
Without additional parameters, \f[B]zelta policy\f[R] will run a
\f[B]zelta backup\f[R] job for each dataset in the configuration file.
Providing one or more operands limits processing to matching jobs.
.PP
Each operand is matched against the following axes for each configured
job:
.TP
\f[B]\f[BI]site\f[B]\f[R]
The user\-defined top\-level grouping in the configuration file.
Selects all jobs belonging to that site.
.TP
\f[B]\f[BI]host\f[B]\f[R]
The source hostname.
Selects all jobs originating from that host.
.TP
\f[B]\f[BI]dataset\f[B]\f[R]
Matches against the source dataset path, the target dataset path, or the
final label (leaf) of either.
For example, \f[CR]bellmgmt_bts\f[R] matches any job whose source or
target ends in that name.
.TP
\f[B]\f[BI]host:dataset\f[B]\f[R]
The fully\-qualified source or target endpoint.
Equivalent to the endpoint format used by \f[B]zelta backup\f[R].
.PP
Multiple operands are matched with OR logic: a job is included if it
matches any operand.
Operands are not matched against \f[B]BACKUP_ROOT\f[R] or other template
values \(em they match against the fully\-resolved endpoint paths that
would be passed to \f[B]zelta backup\f[R].
.SH FILES
For detailed documentation of the \f[B]zelta policy\f[R] configuration
see \f[CR]zelta.conf.example\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]/usr/local/etc/zelta/zelta.conf\f[R]
The default configuration file location.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
For detailed documentation of the \f[B]zelta\f[R] environment variables
see \f[CR]zelta help options\f[R].
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, non\-zero on error.
